# tools/ales_smooth_runs_witness.rish — the run-length smoother: absorb every run shorter than a minimum into what surrounds it (ALES101).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES101.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-000323_fill-ales101-lotus-run-length-smoother.md
# Stands on: lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 — the voice-activity segmenter, whose Segments this reads and rewrites), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 — the Verdict the runs carry).
# ALES95's own scope note named this rung: "a real voice-activity detector adds ... smoothing hysteresis this rung does not — those are later rungs." Raw per-frame
# segmentation flickers — one loud tick reads as a .voiced blip, one quiet frame as a spurious .silent gap; every VAD fixes it the same way: a region must last a minimum
# duration to count, anything shorter absorbed into what surrounds it.
#
# lotus/smooth_runs.rye seeds the output with the first run, then for each later run extends the open run (keeping its verdict) when the run is shorter than min_run OR
# shares the open run's verdict, else pushes it. READ-ONLY over the labels — no PCM touched. The crux is four laws checked as data. COVERAGE: the output tiles the same
# span exactly. COALESCED: no two adjacent output runs share a verdict. HOLD: every run past the first is at least min_run (the opener the honest exception). IDEMPOTENCE
# + MONOTONICITY: a second pass at the same min_run is a fixed point, a larger min_run yields at most as many runs. The selftest proves all four, plus: a spurious short
# blip is absorbed and its neighbours re-coalesce; a leading short run survives; min_run = 0 is the identity; a min_run past the span coalesces to one; empty stays empty
# and a single run is unchanged.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_smooth_runs_witness.rish

say "ales-smooth-runs: Language EN — ALES95's own scope note named this rung: a real voice-activity detector adds smoothing hysteresis this rung does not, those are later rungs. Raw per-frame segmentation flickers — one loud tick inside a pause reads as a one-frame voiced blip, one quiet frame inside speech as a spurious silent gap. Feed that flicker to ALES96's stripper or ALES100's collapser and it splits a pause in two, or spares a click as if it were a word. Every VAD, gate, and auto-splitter fixes this the same way: a region must last a minimum duration to count, anything shorter absorbed into what surrounds it."
say "ales-smooth-runs: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: seed the output with the first run, then for each later run extend the open run (keeping its verdict) when the run is shorter than min_run OR shares the open run's verdict, else push it. A short excursion is relabeled to the surrounding sound and same-verdict neighbours re-coalesce. The crux stands out as four laws checked as data: coverage (the output tiles the same span exactly), coalesced (no adjacent shared verdict), hold (every run past the first at least min_run, the opener the honest exception), and idempotence plus monotonicity."
say "ales-smooth-runs: Lens TAME — a read-only rewrite of one run-length label sequence, not one PCM sample touched; the input contract (a coalesced tiling from ALES95) asserted at the seam rather than re-derived; min_run a plain u32 with no illegal value (0 the identity, a value past the span coalescing to one run); output length can never exceed input length, so SmoothError.SegmentsFull stays named for the honest path though it cannot fire, the same honesty ALES95 keeps; siloed to lotus/, a causal left-to-right hold, NOT the symmetric smoothing or adaptive floors a production VAD would add, not a transcript, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/smooth_runs"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/smooth_runs.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-smooth-runs: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/smooth_runs.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-smooth-runs: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-smooth-runs: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-smooth-runs' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-smooth-runs: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-smooth-runs line"
